               ADLINK SELECTS DIGITAL TO IMPLEMENT NEW
                      VIDEO AD INSERTION SYSTEM


     Major Advertising Interconnect Service Will Distribute Ads
         in Minutes to More Than 50 Cable TV Systems Serving
                        Millions of Customers


MAYNARD, Mass. -- January 11, 1995 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced that Adlink, the second-largest cable advertising 
distribution service in the U.S., has selected Digital to design 
and install a digital video ad insertion system to distribute 
advertising to over 2 million cable TV subscribers in Southern 
California.

     The pilot installation links a video ad insertion system in a 
cable television headend with a Digital Media Studio at Adlink's 
headquarters.  Following the successful implementation of the pilot 
this Spring, Adlink plans to deploy 53 of the Digital systems to its 
affiliated cable companies in the Los Angeles area.  The total 
project is valued at nine million dollars and will represent the 
largest investment made to date by any company for digital video 
ad insertion to replace existing analog equipment.

     According to Charlie Thurston, president and CEO of Adlink, 
"Digital's system cuts the time needed to air new commercials from 
days to minutes.  This opens cable television to new groups of 
advertisers that need to promote time-critical messages, such as 
movie theaters, grocery stores and political campaigns.  Advertisers 
will now be able to run a uniform ad campaign throughout the market 
with customized dealer or franchise information targeted to each 
specific retail trading area.  With Digital's system automating every 
step, we'll not only save money, we'll also gain the accuracy and 
reliability that computerization provides." Thurston added that other 
video programming, such as Adlink's Los Angeles-produced "Local 
Edition" insert in Turner's "Headline News" network, will be digitally 
encoded for distribution.

     "Adlink is exceptionally well-positioned to be a leading 
distributor of all types of video and interactive advertising 
applications," said Sultan Zia, general manager of Digital's Video 
and Interactive Information Services.  "Since Digital's ad insertion 
system is one of many applications that our media servers support, 
Adlink can easily scale-up its equipment to serve a wide range of 
interactive video applications now being developed."

     "Adlink is particularly attracted to Digital's media server 
architecture and capability for future expansion," commented Paul 
Woidke, vice president, director of operations for Adlink.  "The 
system design provides us with the reliability that is needed for 
our remote operations."

     Peter Martin, marketing manager for Digital's video advertising 
systems, stated, "Adlink is an ideal first customer for our new ad 
insertion system.  Because of the large number of local cable TV 
systems it services, the company will benefit from the ad insertion 
system's ability to serve more than 1000 separate channels.  
Digital's ad insertion system is the only one in the industry that 
can expand to meet their rapidly growing business."

     Digital's ad insertion system is based on the company's Video 
and Interactive Information Architecture, which provides a compre-
hensive, standards-based environment for delivering interactive 
video applications over many types of networks.  Currently marketing 
the industry's only second-generation media server, Digital's 
platform features object-oriented middleware that links the world's 
fastest server systems -- based on 64-bit Alpha processors -- with 
the company's StorageWorks disk arrays and digital tape library 
systems.  Digital's ad insertion system uses the MPEG-2 video 
compression standard, which the company helped define, for 
dramatically improved video resolution versus analog technology.

     Adlink is a partnership formed to interconnect the Los Angeles, 
Palm Springs, and Bakersfield cable television systems for 
advertising purposes.  Adlink sells and distributes regional and 
national advertising for insertion on a variety of cable networks, 
including ESPN, CNN, and MTV.  Adlink is one of the fastest growing 
companies in the cable advertising industry.  Its revenues have 
grown from $10.4 million to $26.3 million in the last three years, 
a 153% increase.  The company is equity-owned by TCI, Continental, 
Comcast, Cox, Colony, Crown, KBLCOM, Prime Ticket, King Videocable, 
and DHN Inc.

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry- 
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace.
