                ALCATEL AND DIGITAL SIGN INTERACTIVE
             INFORMATION SERVICES TECHNOLOGY AGREEMENT


   Both Companies Selected For Belgacom's Interactive Video Trial


MAYNARD, Mass. -- January 20, 1995 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
and Alcatel today announced that they have entered a non-exclusive 
cooperative agreement in interactive information and entertainment 
systems.  Under terms of the agreement, the two companies will 
integrate Digital's second-generation media server technology with 
Alcatel's network switching equipment, with Alcatel also providing 
its systems integration expertise to distribute interactive 
information and entertainment services on traditional phone networks 
as well as emerging broadband networks.

     At a press conference held earlier this week in Brussels by 
Belgacom, Belgium's national telecommunications operator, Alcatel 
and Digital stated that their relationship already has led to a 
commercial success.  Both companies, together with Apple as set-top 
provider, were awarded a contract by Belgacom to deliver a complete 
interactive video system for its TECTRIS project, a comprehensive 
interactive video technical trial.

     According to Raymond Polli, Vice President, Network Systems 
Group, Alcatel, "Alcatel and Digital realize that in order to be 
successful in the fast-changing and technically complex world of 
interactive media services, complementary technology and marketing 
alliances must be pursued.  By bringing together the knowledge, 
skills, and competence of both companies, the best interactive video 
delivery team is formed."

     Sultan Zia, general manager of Digital's Video and Interactive 
Information Services, commented, "Alcatel and Digital are ideally 
suited to provide telecommunications and cable companies with 
complete interactive media technology and delivery solutions, 
combining the two companies' product sets and technology expertise.  
We are both excited to participate in Belgacom's first interactive 
video technology trial, and look forward to working with additional 
network providers worldwide in the coming months."

     Commenting on the selection of Alcatel and Digital for its 
first technical trial of interactive video services, Dr. Dirk 
Frimout, Belgacom's director of new developments, stated, "The 
combination of Alcatel and Digital provides the most complete 
interactive video delivery system we have evaluated, including 
options for both ADSL (Asymmetrical Digital Subscriber Line) and 
optical network transmission solutions.  Both companies are unique 
in their ability to provide robust, yet cost-effective 
implementations for small trials that can be easily scaled up to 
larger deployments.  In short, Alcatel's and Digital's combined 
efforts in addressing specific technology challenges make it much 
easier for us to integrate interactive video services on Belgacom's 
network."

     Digital's media server technology has already been selected by 
more cable and telephone companies worldwide than any other media 
server provider.  The company's Video and Interactive Information 
Services Architecture (VIIA) provides a comprehensive, 
standards-based environment for delivering interactive video 
applications over many types of networks.  Currently marketing the 
industry's only second-generation media server, Digital's platform 
features object-oriented middleware that links the world's fastest 
server systems -- based on 64-bit Alpha processors -- with the 
company's StorageWorks disk arrays and digital tape library systems.

     Alcatel has gained a leading position as a supplier of 
broadband- based ATM (Asynchronous Transfer Mode) switching and 
access networks.  Its network equipment, including the A1000 ATM 
Switch, and Alcatel's broadband access networks, provide essential 
components of the information highway of tomorrow.

     Alcatel's business spans the entire scope of communications 
systems.  These include public switching and line transmission 
systems along with network design and integration, mobile 
communication, radio transmission systems, space and defense, 
business communications, power and telecommunications, cable and 
transport automation systems.

