                       DIGITAL ANNOUNCES 1995
                      HMO PERFORMANCE STANDARDS


MAYNARD, Mass. -- January 23, 1995 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
recently released an enhanced HMO Performance Standards document to 
approximately 100 health care providers and partners across the 
United States.  The standards are designed to clarify expectations 
for HMOs participating in the Digital network, and to continuously 
improve the quality, efficiency and overall value of the services 
available to Digital's HMO subscribers.  

Enhancements to the Performance Standards include:

     o 	Requirement of participating HMO accreditation by the NCQA
     o 	Incorporation of HIV/AIDS standards
     o 	Greater emphasis on the use of information technology in
       	medical management
     o 	New time frame for symptomatic office visits from 72 hours 
        to 48 hours
     o 	Availability of gerontologists, rheumatologists and 
       	podiatrists
     o 	Incorporation of other national standards

     With its HMO Network Manager -- John Hancock -- Digital works 
in partnership with its HMOs to develop quality improvement goals, 
then monitors and measures HMO progress and compliance with the 
Performance Standards over time.  As a result, HMOs are held 
accountable for the quality and cost of care delivered to Digital 
and its employees.  

     The 1995 HMO Performance Standards are an enhancement to the 
original version released in January of 1991.  The new version 
incorporates best practices, industry models and goals for the 
future, while continuing to emphasize quality improvement, 
partnership and a focus on the delivery system.  Digital's 
Performance Standards are organized in five categories:

     o  Access/Administration/Member Services
     o  Clinical Quality Standards
     o  Behavioral Health Standards
     o  Information Reporting and Management
     o  Finance and Contracts

     "The new HMO Performance Standards reflect Digital's commitment 
to hold HMOs and the delivery system accountable for the quality and 
cost of care delivered to our employees," says Fran Bastien, U.S. 
Health/Disability Benefits Manager at Digital.  "We believe that by 
focusing on the drivers of health care costs and by encouraging our 
partners to better integrate services, HMOs can deliver the highest 
quality care at the best prices with optimal outcomes.  This 
approach -- which focuses on the organized systems of care concept 
-- was put forth through collaborative efforts with the Washington 
Business Group on Health."  

     "Digital is revolutionary in their approach to performance 
expectations," says Mary Jane England, President of the Washington 
Business Group on Health.  "They are working in partnership with 
their health care providers to continually improve the performance 
standards with substantial consumer input.  Their work benefits the 
entire industry and serves as a model for improving the quality of 
services delivered through managed care."

Collaborative Effort

     The 1995 standards were developed through a process that 
unfolded over two years and involved input from numerous health 
plans.  Staff from Digital and Digital's Network Manager, John 
Hancock, formed teams of specialty areas that conformed with each 
section of the 1991 standards.  Each team drafted standards for 
their specific area using experience gained since 1991 and 
incorporating information from other sets of standards.  A draft 
was sent to Digital's HMO partners, who provided considerable 
feedback that was incorporated into the final document.  

HMOs who reviewed and provided input to Digital's Standards of 
Care are:

     o CIGNA			       o Fallon
     o Harvard Community Health        o HMO Blue
       Plan			       o Kaiser
     o Kaiser-North California	       o Lovelace
     o Matthew Thornton		       o Takecare-Colorado
     o Tufts			       o US Healthcare

Background

     Digital has responded to escalating health care costs by 
becoming an active and creative purchaser of health care services.  
Digital has designed its current health care strategy around a model 
that encourages partnerships with well-organized, well-managed, 
efficient HMOs.  

     Digital is committed to maintaining a healthy and productive 
workforce, and has been successful in providing a reasonable choice 
of high-quality, cost effective health plans to a diverse U.S. 
workforce made up of approximately 30,000 individuals.

     Since its implementation in 1991, the managed care strategy has 
contributed significantly to controlling health care costs, while 
maintaining the quality of care provided to employees and their 
dependents, Bastien notes.  "Total savings since the strategy was 
implemented approaches $100 million," he says.  "Additionally, 
results of a recent employee satisfaction survey indicated that 
employees in HMOs were significantly more satisfied with the overall 
care they received than employees enrolled in traditional 
fee-for-service plans."  

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace. 
